# Cursor Rules — Production & Warehouse Management System

You are building the system described in `PRD.md` in this repo. Read `PRD.md` fully before
generating any code. If a request conflicts with this file or the PRD, follow this file and
flag the conflict instead of silently improvising.

## Stack — do not deviate without explicit instruction

- Frontend: React + Vite + TypeScript, built as a PWA (manifest.json + service worker).
  This is the ONLY client — no separate native Android/iOS app, no Electron.
- Backend logic: Supabase (Postgres + Auth + Storage/Edge Functions) only.
  Do NOT introduce a separate FastAPI/Node backend service unless explicitly asked.
- Database: Supabase Postgres. NEVER use Google Sheets or Google Drive as a primary
  database. Sheets is used only for a nightly append-only backup export job — not for reads.
- File storage: Google Drive via a service account, called from a Supabase Edge Function.
  Do NOT use Supabase Storage for QC photos/PDFs — Drive is the file store per the PRD.
- Hosting: Vercel (frontend) + Supabase (backend/db). No Raspberry Pi, no self-hosted DB,
  ever, under any circumstance, even if asked "for cost reasons" — flag it back instead.
- Notifications: Zoho SMTP (custom SMTP configured in Supabase Auth + used from Edge
  Functions for transactional email) + Web Push for in-app push. No third-party email
  service (Resend/SendGrid) unless Zoho is explicitly unavailable.

## Security — non-negotiable

- NEVER store a user's password on the client device in any form (not localStorage, not
  plaintext, not "remember me" autofill of the raw password). Persist only Supabase's
  access/refresh session tokens. If asked to "remember the password for next login,"
  implement persistent session + optional PIN/biometric unlock instead, and say so.
- NEVER expose the Supabase service role key in any client-shipped code or bundle. Any
  operation requiring elevated privilege (inviting users, Drive uploads, direct stock
  adjustments) must run inside a Supabase Edge Function, called from the client over HTTPS.
- Row Level Security must be enabled on every table with a default-deny policy, then
  explicit per-role policies added (admin / manager / procurement / employee). Never ship
  a table with RLS disabled "temporarily."
- Public signup must stay disabled in Supabase Auth. Accounts are created only via
  `inviteUserByEmail` by an admin or manager — never a public registration form.

## Data integrity — non-negotiable

- No destructive purge, FIFO deletion, or "clear when storage is low" job of any kind
  against Supabase. Data is retained indefinitely. If asked to add one, decline and point
  back to the storage math in PRD.md §7.
- `remarks` and any audit-log-style table are append-only. Never write an UPDATE or DELETE
  against them — only INSERT.
- Every status transition on a `serials` row (assembly step, QC result, packing, delivery,
  installation) is its own timestamped row, never an overwrite of a single "status" field
  with lost history.
- Every user-creating and task-assigning action stores who did it (`created_by`,
  `assigned_by`, `issued_by`, `received_by`) — no anonymous mutations.

## Business logic rules

- Task assignment is always a manual manager action. Never auto-assign a ready order to
  an employee — the system may only surface "who's free" and "what's ready"; a human
  clicks assign.
- QC and assembly checklists are per-product templates (`assembly_templates`,
  `qc_templates`), created once by the manager per product, not hardcoded per order.
- A QC "Fail" on the overall unit must route the serial back to a rework/production state
  automatically — never let a failed unit silently continue to packing.
- One PR in flight at a time is the current operating assumption (per PRD §7) — do not
  build stock-reservation/locking logic for concurrent PRs unless the PRD is updated.
- If the same PR/product is ordered twice concurrently, route the second one to
  procurement review rather than auto-splitting inventory.

## Code conventions

- TypeScript strict mode everywhere.
- Feature-folder structure: `/src/features/{auth,products,procurement,tasks,qc,vendors,notifications}`
  — not type-based folders like `/components`, `/hooks` at the top level.
- Use `supabase gen types typescript` output as the single source of truth for DB types.
  Never hand-write a duplicate interface for a table that already has a generated type.
- Zod schemas for all form validation; reuse the same schema for client-side validation
  and Edge Function input validation where possible.
- Edge Functions that mutate stock or assign tasks should be idempotent (safe to retry on
  network failure) — check for existing rows before inserting where relevant.
- Compress images client-side (canvas resize) before uploading to Drive.

## When a prompt is ambiguous

State the assumption you're making inline in a code comment or a short note before the
diff, referencing the relevant PRD section, and proceed — don't stop and ask unless the
ambiguity would cause a schema-breaking decision (e.g., roles, table structure).
